  "cover_question": "游戏风靡世界后，为什么作者多年未分到游戏收益？",
  "reading_note": "",
  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE-SPECIFIC SCENE: A coherent still life on a modest late-1980s publishing-office meeting table: an unbranded upright gray pocket handheld game console, inspired by the original Game Boy, is the clear focal object. The console has a large muted-green monochrome screen, a simple cross-shaped direction pad and two plain dark terracotta round buttons with no letters. On screen show only a few simple falling geometric clusters, each composed of exactly four square cells, and a sparse bottom stack; absolutely no scores, interface text or logos. One plain black floppy disk and a small stack of completely blank licensing papers are the only supporting objects, linking Soviet software circulation with the later handheld-rights deal. The scene evokes the move from floppy copies to a mass-market pocket game, not a fake historical photograph or a collage. Do not show the Elektronika 60 displaying colorful graphics, and do not print character-based screens because no typography is allowed. Natural soft window light, warm ivory paper, sage display, restrained terracotta accents.",
